                        title: 'Building Blocks of Civilization',
                        body: 'Despite the costs, farming created the conditions for everything that followed. Food surpluses allowed the rise of villages, towns, and empires. They enabled specialists: kings, scribes, priests, and artisans. They required the invention of writing and mathematics to track taxes and grain. The "imagined orders" of the Core Thesis could now scale to millions.',
                        prompt: "THE ULTIMATE CONSEQUENCE",
                        image: 'images/sapiens/3_4.png',
                        details: `<p>Despite the costs, farming created the conditions for everything that followed. Food surpluses allowed the rise of villages, towns, and empires. They enabled specialists: kings, scribes, priests, and artisans. They required the invention of writing and mathematics to track taxes and grain. The "imagined orders" of the Core Thesis could now scale to millions.</p>
                        <p>Despite the misery it brought to the average person, the Agricultural Revolution created the conditions for the modern world. Food surpluses and permanent settlements allowed for the growth of villages, towns, and eventually massive empires.</p>
                        <p>For the first time, not everyone had to be a food producer. This allowed the rise of <strong>specialists</strong>\u2014kings, soldiers, scribes, priests, and artisans. Writing and mathematics were invented not for poetry, but to track taxes, grain surpluses, and land ownership. The "imagined orders" from the Core Thesis could now scale up: large-scale kingdoms, organized religions, and complex legal codes could only be built on the foundation of a large, settled, agricultural population.</p>`
