                        eyebrow: 'THE PARADOX OF ALTRUISM',
                        title: 'The Calculated Sacrifice',
                        body: 'If genes are selfish, why would a mother deer risk her life to save her fawn? It looks like kindness, but it is actually a cold calculation. Because the baby carries 50% of the mother\'s genes, saving the baby is a strategic move to ensure those genes survive. The mother acts "altruistically" only because her genes are acting "selfishly" to protect copies of themselves.',
                        prompt: "KIN SELECTION",
                        image: 'images/selfish_gene/1_3.png',
                        details: `<p>If genes are selfish, why would a mother deer risk her life to save her fawn? It looks like kindness, but it is actually a cold calculation. Because the baby carries 50% of the mother\'s genes, saving the baby is a strategic move to ensure those genes survive. The mother acts "altruistically" only because her genes are acting "selfishly" to protect copies of themselves.</p>
                        <p>If every gene is "selfish"—programmed only to maximize its own replication—then why do we see apparent kindness in nature? A mother bird pretends to have a broken wing to lead a predator away from her nest. A worker bee stings an intruder, sacrificing its own life.</p>
                        <p>Dawkins explains this through <strong>Kin Selection</strong>. The mother bird's babies carry 50% of her genes. By risking her life, she potentially saves multiple copies of her genes. The math works out: if she saves three babies (each carrying 50%), she's preserved 150% of her gene copies at the risk of only 100% (herself). Her "altruism" is her genes' cold, mathematical selfishness.</p>`
