                        details: `<p>Why do wolves hunt together and birds feed their siblings? It’s called Kin Selection. Since relatives share a portion of your DNA, helping them survive is actually a way of helping your own genes survive. It explains why family bonds are so strong in nature: it’s the genes protecting their own copies residing in someone else’s body.</p>
                        <p>Kin Selection, formalized by biologist W.D. Hamilton, explains why family bonds are so strong across the animal kingdom. The rule is simple: an organism will help a relative if the cost to itself is less than the benefit to the relative, weighted by their degree of genetic relatedness.</p>
                        <p>You share 50% of your genes with a sibling, 25% with a nephew, and 12.5% with a first cousin. This is why, as the biologist J.B.S. Haldane famously quipped, "I would lay down my life for two brothers or eight cousins." The math of gene survival explains the intensity of family love—and also why conflicts with strangers are so much easier than conflicts with family.</p>`
