                        eyebrow: 'THE MEME',
                        title: 'Ideas Are Alive - Memes',
                        body: 'Dawkins asks: Is DNA the only thing that replicates? No! He introduces the Meme: an idea, a tune, or a fashion trend. Just like genes leap from body to body, memes leap from brain to brain through imitation. If a song is catchy, it spreads. This is the bridge to human culture—the "imagined orders" we learned about in Sapiens are actually giant webs of memes!',
                        prompt: "THE NEW REPLICATOR",
                        image: 'images/selfish_gene/2_3.png',
                        details: `<p>Dawkins asks: Is DNA the only thing that replicates? No! He introduces the Meme: an idea, a tune, or a fashion trend. Just like genes leap from body to body, memes leap from brain to brain through imitation. If a song is catchy, it spreads. This is the bridge to human culture—the "imagined orders" we learned about in Sapiens are actually giant webs of memes!</p>
                        <p>In the final chapter, Dawkins introduces one of the most influential ideas in modern culture: the <strong>meme</strong>. Just as a gene is a replicator in the biological world, a meme is a replicator in the cultural world. It is any unit of cultural information—a tune, a catchphrase, a fashion, a recipe, a religious belief—that spreads from brain to brain through imitation.</p>
                        <p>Memes follow the same Darwinian rules as genes: they vary, they replicate, and they compete for limited resources (your attention and memory). The "fittest" memes—the catchiest tunes, the most compelling stories—spread and survive. The weak ones are forgotten. This is the biological foundation for understanding culture, and it directly connects to Sapiens' "imagined orders."</p>`
