                        prompt: "THE BIG MISTAKE",
                        image: 'images/selfish_gene/3_1.png',
                        details: `<p>The most important misunderstanding to correct: Dawkins is NOT saying people are inherently selfish or that selfishness is good. He is describing genes as behaving "as if" they are selfish\u2014a metaphor for their tendency to maximize their own replication. This says nothing about how humans should behave.</p>
                        <p>This is the single most misunderstood point about the book. Dawkins has repeatedly emphasized that calling genes "selfish" is a <strong>metaphor</strong>. Genes don't have feelings, intentions, or desires. They are just molecules.</p>
                        <p>Crucially, selfish genes can and do produce generous, cooperative, and altruistic organisms. In fact, being generous to your relatives is one of the most effective strategies a "selfish" gene can employ. The book's title describes the gene, not the person.</p>`
