                        <p>Your brain is a general-purpose data processing machine. It doesn't "know" what eyes or ears are—it just knows how to process streams of electrical data. This means you can swap the inputs. In real experiments, researchers have fed visual data to the brain through vibrations on the tongue or skin, and over time the brain learned to "see" through those channels.</p>
