                        <p>Mirror neurons were first discovered in macaque monkeys and have since been identified in humans. These remarkable cells fire both when you do something <em>and</em> when you observe someone else doing the same thing. When you watch someone reach for a glass, the same motor neurons fire in your brain as if you were reaching for it yourself.</p>
