                        prompt: "THE COGNITIVE COST",
                        image: 'images/zebras/3_3.png',
                        details: `<p>The hippocampus\u2014a brain region critical for learning and memory\u2014is packed with cortisol receptors. Chronic exposure to high cortisol is toxic to its neurons. It can damage them, prevent new ones from forming, and even cause the region to physically shrink. The consequence: impaired memory, difficulty learning, and an increased risk of major depression.</p>
                        <p>This links directly to our discussion of The Brain. The <strong>hippocampus</strong>\u2014the brain region critical for learning, memory formation, and mood regulation\u2014is packed with cortisol receptors, making it extremely sensitive to stress.</p>
                        <p>Chronic exposure to high cortisol levels is literally <strong>toxic to hippocampal neurons</strong>. It can damage existing neurons, prevent the formation of new ones (neurogenesis), and even cause the entire region to physically shrink. The consequences are devastating: impaired memory, difficulty learning new things, and\u2014because a healthy hippocampus plays a key role in regulating mood\u2014a significantly increased risk of major depression. This is a direct, physical link between chronic stress and cognitive decline.</p>`
