                        body: 'Neuroplasticity means experience doesn\'t just happen to you\u2014it gets physically encoded in your brain. Every skill you practice strengthens a neural pathway. But trauma can "burn" a fear memory into your amygdala, creating a hyper-sensitive alarm system that stays on long after the danger has passed\u2014the biological basis of PTSD.',
                        prompt: "PLASTICITY & TRAUMA",
                        image: 'images/behave/3_1.png',
                        details: `<p>Neuroplasticity means experience doesn\'t just happen to you\u2014it gets physically encoded in your brain. Every skill you practice strengthens a neural pathway. But trauma can "burn" a fear memory into your amygdala, creating a hyper-sensitive alarm system that stays on long after the danger has passed\u2014the biological basis of PTSD.</p>
                        <p>As we learned from The Brain, neuroplasticity means experience physically reshapes your neural circuits. Every skill practiced strengthens specific pathways. "Use it or lose it" is the fundamental law. The person you are today is a reflection of the circuits you've spent years strengthening.</p>
                        <p>But plasticity isn't always positive. <strong>Trauma</strong> is the brain learning a lesson too well and getting stuck. A terrifying event floods the brain with stress hormones, "burning" the memory into the amygdala. This creates a hyper-sensitive fear circuit that fires at the slightest reminder. The frontal cortex, which should calm the amygdala, can go offline when triggered. The person's brain is physically stuck in the state of the original trauma\u2014this is the neurobiology of <strong>PTSD</strong>.</p>`
