                        prompt: "THE FORMATIVE YEARS",
                        image: 'images/behave/3_2.png',
                        details: `<p>Early childhood adversity isn\'t just an unhappy memory\u2014it\'s a biological event. It can permanently set the stress system\'s volume too high and impair frontal cortex development. In adolescence, there\'s a critical mismatch: the emotional gas pedal (limbic system) is fully mature, but the brakes (frontal cortex) don\'t finish wiring until your mid-20s. Gas pedal: full power. Brakes: under construction.</p>
                        <p><strong>Childhood:</strong> Early adversity\u2014trauma, neglect, poverty\u2014is not just an unhappy memory. It is a biological event with lifelong consequences. It can permanently set the stress-response "volume" too high, creating an adult with a hyper-reactive amygdala and chronically elevated cortisol. It can impair development of the frontal cortex, leading to lifelong challenges with impulse control.</p>
                        <p><strong>Adolescence:</strong> The classic teen behaviors aren't character flaws\u2014they're a predictable neurological mismatch. The emotional, reward-seeking limbic system is fully mature and online. But the frontal cortex\u2014responsible for impulse control and long-term planning\u2014is the <strong>very last part of the brain to mature</strong>, not completing its wiring until the mid-20s. It's a car with a powerful engine and faulty brakes.</p>`
