                        title: 'The Trouble with Categories',
                        body: 'Our System 1 loves neat, binary buckets: good vs. evil, us vs. them, nature vs. nurture. Sapolsky\'s primary argument is that biology is almost never categorical\u2014it\'s a world of continuums. There is no "aggression gene," no "love hormone," no single "fear center." Every label is a simplification that obscures the messy, complex truth.',
                        prompt: "BEYOND BINARY",
                        image: 'images/behave/4_1.png',
                        details: `<p>Our System 1 loves neat, binary buckets: good vs. evil, us vs. them, nature vs. nurture. Sapolsky\'s primary argument is that biology is almost never categorical\u2014it\'s a world of continuums. There is no "aggression gene," no "love hormone," no single "fear center." Every label is a simplification that obscures the messy, complex truth.</p>
                        <p>Our brains (System 1) love to think in neat, binary buckets: good vs. evil, us vs. them, nature vs. nurture. Sapolsky's primary argument is that biology is almost never categorical; it's a world of <strong>continuums</strong>.</p>
                        <p>Nature vs. Nurture? A false dichotomy\u2014every gene's expression is influenced by the environment, and every environment is interpreted through genes. A "good" or "bad" hormone? Oxytocin isn't the "love hormone"; it's the "pro-us" hormone. Testosterone isn't the "aggression hormone"; it's a "status-seeking" hormone. A single brain region's "function"? The amygdala isn't just the "fear center." The lesson: <strong>resist your brain's desire for simple labels</strong> and embrace the messy, continuous nature of biology.</p>`
